    {
        "content": "<p>Why do you want this? In the vast majority of cases we only want one of <code>p ↔ q</code> or <code>q ↔ p</code> in Mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 583855587,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1775503337
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And the general rule is that the RHS should be \"simpler\" than the RHS, such as in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finset.eq_iff_card_le_of_subset#doc\">docs#Finset.eq_iff_card_le_of_subset</a>.</p>",
        "id": 583855674,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1775503379
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/symmetry.20of.20iff.20theorem.20with.20.28many.29.20hypothesis/near/583855587\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why do you want this? In the vast majority of cases we only want one of <code>p ↔ q</code> or <code>q ↔ p</code> in Mathlib.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>it would be more convenient than doing <code>have</code> statements filling in the hypothesis or defining them as their own theorems , what is described by Yan is not what I am looking for. I don't want to even have to specify the arguments and pass them to the theorem and then apply <code>Iff.comm</code> using simpa on the result.</p>\n<p>something like <code>simpa [simp_lemma] some_theorem</code> , I haven't used <code>simpa</code> before so learned something new nonetheless.</p>",
