        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"714864\">Jad Abou Hawili</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/symmetry.20of.20iff.20theorem.20with.20.28many.29.20hypothesis/near/583862850\">said</a> in <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/symmetry.20of.20iff.20theorem.20with.20.28many.29.20hypothesis/with/583862850\"><a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"113489\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/symmetry.20of.20iff.20theorem.20with.20.28many.29.20hypothesis/with/583862850\">#new members &gt; symmetry of iff theorem with (many) hypothesis</a></a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/symmetry.20of.20iff.20theorem.20with.20.28many.29.20hypothesis/near/583855587\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why do you want this? In the vast majority of cases we only want one of <code>p ↔ q</code> or <code>q ↔ p</code> in Mathlib.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>it would be more convenient then doing have statements filling in the hypothesis or defining them as their own theorems , what is described by Yan is not what I am looking for. I don't want to even have to specify the arguments and pass them to the theorem and then apply <code>Iff.comm</code> using simpa on the result.</p>\n<p>something like <code>simpa [simp_lemma] some_theorem</code> , I haven't used <code>simpa</code> before so learned something new nonetheless.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>some_theorem</code> would be something with many hypothesis but I wouldn't have to do <code>fun a1 a2 a3 ...</code> , this is what i'm looking for</p>",
